---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "How Much Does an Incline Trainer Cost? (2026): X22i, X24 and More";
const description =
  "A true incline trainer costs $2,875 to $4,299 for home use (NordicTrack X16 $3,699, X24 $4,299), plus $39 a month for iFIT Pro. Commercial ones top $10,000.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "By Price", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "How Much Does an Incline Trainer Cost?", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/how-much-does-an-incline-trainer-cost/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does an incline trainer cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A powered home incline trainer costs about $2,875 to $4,299 in November 2026: $2,875 for the FreeMotion i11.9 and $2,999.99 for the Sunny Versatile on Amazon (23 November), and $3,699 for the NordicTrack X16 and $4,299 for the X24 on NordicTrack's checkout (5 November). Commercial models cost $10,000 or more.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is a NordicTrack X22i?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The X22i is no longer sold new: NordicTrack has replaced it and the X32i with the X16 ($3,699) and X24 ($4,299). A used X22i's fair price depends on age and condition; test the console, incline and decline before buying, because a replacement console for an older X-series trainer can cost over $1,400.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you need iFIT for a NordicTrack incline trainer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not to use it as a treadmill, but the classes and filmed routes that move the deck automatically need iFIT Pro, which is $39 a month or $396 a year at iFIT's regular prices on 23 November 2026. Over five years that is $1,980 to $2,340 on top of the machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is an incline trainer worth the extra money over a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only if you will regularly walk above 15%. An X16 costs $1,700 more than a NordicTrack Commercial 1750 with the same screen size, and the extra buys 40% incline, a -6% decline and a 400 lb frame, not a better running treadmill.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the cheapest incline trainer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The cheapest machines reaching 40% are manual walking treadmills such as the LONTEK ($195.49) and CURSOR ($249.99) on Amazon on 23 November 2026, but you set their incline by hand. The cheapest powered 30% trainer we found was the FreeMotion i11.9 at $2,875.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How tall a ceiling does an incline trainer need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Run to the Finish reports NordicTrack's guidance as the user's height plus three feet, so about nine feet for a six-foot user. The X24's deck rises from about 14 inches flat to 31.5 inches at full incline in Treadmill Review Guru's measurement.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-23",
      dateModified: "2026-11-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/how-much-does-an-incline-trainer-cost/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "price");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const GROUP_PAGES = GROUP?.pages ?? [];
const HERE_AT = GROUP_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.url === HERE);
// The group's first 2 pages (its leads), then the pages after this one, wrapping round, so every page in a long
// hub group gets sibling links and the leads keep theirs (PLAYBOOK §0b L1).
const MORE = [...new Set([...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, 2), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(HERE_AT + 1), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, Math.max(HERE_AT, 0))])].filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-23",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "how much does an incline trainer cost",
      "incline trainer price",
      "nordictrack x22i price",
      "nordictrack x24 price",
      "nordictrack x16 price",
      "incline trainer cost",
      "nordictrack incline trainer price",
      "40% incline treadmill price",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Incline Trainer Prices", link: "#price-table" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/price/">By Price</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Price Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">How Much Does an Incline Trainer Cost?</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A home incline trainer that climbs to 30% or 40% with a powered deck costs about $2,875 to $4,299: $3,699 for the NordicTrack X16 and $4,299 for the X24 on NordicTrack’s checkout, and $2,875 for the FreeMotion i11.9 on Amazon. iFIT Pro adds $39 a month on the NordicTrack models, and commercial trainers from TRUE and Freemotion cost more than $10,000. Here is every price, the five-year total, and the cheaper ways to walk uphill.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 23, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on retailer and maker listings, with prices checked on the dates shown.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#price-table" class="btn-ghost">Prices by Model</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          An incline trainer is a treadmill built to climb: 30% or 40% where an ordinary treadmill stops at 12 or 15%,
          usually with a decline as well. That lift system, the heavier frame it needs and the big screen most of them
          carry put incline trainers near the top of the home treadmill market. On 23 November 2026 the cheapest powered
          machine reaching 30% on Amazon was the FreeMotion i11.9 at $2,875, the 45% Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Versatile
          Treadmill was $2,999.99, and NordicTrack&rsquo;s X16 and X24, the incline trainers most people mean, were $3,699
          and $4,299 on NordicTrack&rsquo;s own checkout when we last read it. The X22i and X32i, the names many people still
          search for, have been replaced by the X16 and X24. This page sets out what each costs, what the extra money buys
          over a 15% treadmill, what iFIT adds, and the costs people forget: delivery, assembly, wiring and a ceiling tall
          enough to use the top of the range.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#price-table">Incline Trainer Prices by Model</a></li>
            <li><a href="#tiers">The Four Price Tiers, Explained</a></li>
            <li><a href="#vs-15-percent">What the Extra Money Buys Over a 15% Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#ifit">iFIT Membership: The Cost on Top</a></li>
            <li><a href="#five-year">The Five-Year Cost of an Incline Trainer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#x22i-x32i">What About the X22i, X32i and X9i?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#x16-vs-x24">X16 or X24: Is the Bigger Screen Worth $600?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#delivery-space">Delivery, Assembly, Space and Ceiling Height</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty">Warranty and Repair Costs</a></li>
            <li><a href="#alternatives">Cheaper Alternatives: High-Incline Treadmills</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-needs">Who Should Pay for an Incline Trainer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when">When Incline Trainers Are Cheapest</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A home incline trainer with a powered 30% to 45% incline costs about $2,875 to $4,299 in November
            2026. NordicTrack&rsquo;s X16 is $3,699 and the X24 $4,299 on NordicTrack&rsquo;s checkout (5 November 2026), and
            both need iFIT Pro at $39 a month for the classes. Commercial incline trainers cost $10,000 or more.</strong> A manual 40% walking treadmill can be had for about $200, but it is not the same machine.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Manual 40% walking treadmills:</strong> about $195 to $250 on Amazon (23 November 2026); incline set by
            hand in five steps, 6.2 mph top speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Powered 30% to 45% for home:</strong> $2,875 (<a href="/freemotion-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>FreeMotion</a> i11.9) and $2,999.99 (Sunny Versatile) on
            Amazon, 23 November 2026.</li>
            <li><strong>NordicTrack X16 and X24 (-6% to 40%):</strong> $3,699 and $4,299 on NordicTrack&rsquo;s checkout,
            5 November 2026; not sold new on Amazon US.</li>
            <li><strong>iFIT Pro:</strong> $39 a month or $396 a year (iFIT.com, 23 November 2026), so $1,980 to $2,340 over
            five years.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial (TRUE Alpine Runner, Freemotion i22.9):</strong> from about $10,300 to $11,899.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are not sure you need more than 15%, read <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainer vs treadmill</a> first; the machines themselves are compared in our <a href="/best-incline-trainers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline trainers</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Incline Trainer Prices by Model</h2>
<p>
            Prices are from the Amazon listing or the maker&rsquo;s own site on the date shown. NordicTrack&rsquo;s website
            could not be read on 23 November 2026 (it refused automated requests), so its prices are the ones its checkout
            pages showed on 5 November 2026, when we checked them for our X24 review. Expect them to move around Black
            Friday on 27 November.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[680px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machine</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Source and date</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Incline range</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Speed</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">LONTEK 40% Incline Treadmill (manual)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$195.49</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon, 23 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–40% in 5 manual steps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.6–6.2 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">CURSOR 40% Incline Treadmill (manual)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$249.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon, 23 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–40% in 5 manual steps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.6–6.2 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FreeMotion i11.9 Incline Trainer</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,875</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon, 23 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30% incline and decline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Versatile Treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,999.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon, 23 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">45% in the title (bullets say 50%) with decline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.3–5 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack X16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack checkout, 5 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-6% to 40%, powered</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–12 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack X24</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,299</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack checkout, 5 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-6% to 40%, powered</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–12 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack X22i / X32i</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No new price</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replaced by the X16 and X24</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steep incline with decline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">See our X22i review</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRUE Alpine Runner (commercial)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">From about $10,300</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRUE dealers, 6 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30% with decline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">See maker</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Freemotion i22.9 (commercial)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$11,899</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Freemotion store, 6 Nov 2026</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30% with decline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 mph</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Specifications are as stated by each Amazon listing or, for the NordicTrack X-series, by NordicTrack and the
            independent reviews summarised in our <a href="/nordictrack-x24-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack X24 review</a>.
            The Sunny&rsquo;s listing disagrees with itself: its title says 45% incline, its bullets say up to 50% with a -5%
            decline. Its price has also risen: it was $2,199.99 on Amazon on 6 November and $2,999.99 on 23 November. The
            FreeMotion i11.9 listing gives no top speed, weight rating or warranty. The TRUE and Freemotion commercial
            prices are the figures we found for our incline trainer guide on 6 November 2026; neither is sold on Amazon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Four Price Tiers, Explained</h2>
<p>
            &ldquo;Incline trainer&rdquo; covers machines that differ by a factor of fifty in price. They fall into four
            groups, and knowing which one you are shopping in saves a lot of confusion.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>About $200: manual 40% walking treadmills.</strong> The CURSOR and LONTEK listings describe a
            walking-pad-style machine with tall handrails and a deck you prop up to 0, 10, 20, 30 or 40% by hand, reaching
            about 15 inches at the front. Top speed is 6.2 mph, the belts are 38.6 by 15.4 inches and both are rated to 350 lb.
            You cannot change the grade mid-walk, and the belt is far shorter and narrower than an incline trainer&rsquo;s.
            They are a cheap way to find out whether steep walking suits you, not a substitute for a powered trainer.</li>
            <li><strong>About $2,900 to $3,000: powered steep-grade machines on Amazon.</strong> The FreeMotion i11.9 is an
            older 30% trainer from iFIT&rsquo;s commercial brand (its listing still mentions iFit Live, the earlier name for
            iFIT), and the Sunny Versatile is a walking and hiking machine that stops at 5 mph. Both change grade with a
            motor; neither is a running machine at steep grades.</li>
            <li><strong>About $3,700 to $4,300: NordicTrack X16 and X24.</strong> The mainstream home incline trainer: -6% to
            40%, 12 mph, a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.25 CHP motor and a 400 lb rating, by NordicTrack&rsquo;s figures, with a 16- or
            24-inch touchscreen. Sold direct by NordicTrack, not on Amazon US.</li>
            <li><strong>$10,000 and up: commercial incline trainers.</strong> TRUE&rsquo;s Alpine Runner and Freemotion&rsquo;s
            i22.9 are built for gyms and sold through dealers or the maker&rsquo;s store. TRUE gives 592 lb for the Alpine Runner
            and a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit. Few homes need one.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For most buyers the real choice is between the X16 at $3,699 and a good 15% treadmill at a third to a half of
            that price, which is the comparison the next section makes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-15-percent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Extra Money Buys Over a 15% Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack makes the cleanest comparison possible, because it sells incline trainers and ordinary treadmills
            with the same screen sizes. The X16 and the Commercial 1750 both have 16-inch touchscreens; the X24 and the
            Commercial 2450 both have 24-inch ones. The price difference is, roughly, the price of the steep incline.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[620px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pair</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Incline trainer</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Standard treadmill</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Difference</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16-inch screen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X16: $3,699, -6% to 40%, non-folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 1750: $1,999, -3% to 12%, 12 mph, folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,700</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24-inch screen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X24: $4,299, -6% to 40%, non-folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 2450: $2,499, -3% to 12%, 14 mph, folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,800</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No subscription, 15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X16: $3,699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F65: $1,199.99, 0–15%, 12 mph, 20 x 60 in., folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,499</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            X16 and X24 prices from NordicTrack&rsquo;s checkout on 5 November 2026; Commercial 1750, Commercial 2450 and Sole
            F65 prices and specifications from their Amazon listings on 23 November 2026. So about $1,700 to $1,800 buys:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>28 more percentage points of incline.</strong> 40% is a little under 22 degrees; 12% is under 7. At
            walking pace that is the difference between a stiff road hill and a mountain trail. Our <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline percent vs degrees</a> guide converts the figures.</li>
            <li><strong>A deeper decline,</strong> -6% against -3%, for downhill training.</li>
            <li><strong>A heavier, non-folding frame</strong> rated to 400 lb by NordicTrack.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What it does not buy is a better running treadmill. Treadmill Review Guru found the X24&rsquo;s top speed capped
            at 8 mph above 16% incline and at 6 mph from 25% upward, and the Commercial 2450 reaches 14 mph to the X24&rsquo;s 12.
            If your sessions are flat or under 15%, the extra $1,700 buys nothing you will use. The <a href="/sole-f65-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F65</a> and <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1750</a> reviews cover the alternatives in full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>iFIT Membership: The Cost on Top</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack&rsquo;s incline trainers are built around iFIT. The trainer-led and filmed-route workouts change the
            speed and grade for you, which on a 40% machine is most of the appeal: the deck tilts to follow the trail on the
            screen. That needs iFIT Pro, the tier for equipment with built-in touchscreens from NordicTrack, ProForm and
            Freemotion.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
