          </p>
          <p>
            The FreeMotion and Sunny totals assume you use their built-in programs; the i11.9&rsquo;s iFit Live programming
            dates from an earlier generation of iFIT, so ask the seller whether its connected features still work before
            counting on them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="x22i-x32i" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What About the X22i, X32i and X9i?</h2>
<p>
            Many people searching for incline trainer prices are looking for the NordicTrack X22i or X32i, the models that
            made the category famous. NordicTrack has replaced them on its site with the X16 and X24, and neither was listed
            new on Amazon US on 23 November 2026; searches there returned only replacement parts. The X9i is an earlier,
            smaller-screen incline trainer in the same family.
          </p>
          <p>
            That leaves the second-hand market, where X22i and X9i machines turn up regularly. There is no single fair price
            for a used one, because condition and age vary so much, but a few figures help you judge a listing. On Amazon on
            23 November 2026:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>a replacement walking belt for the Commercial X22i was $139.99;</li>
            <li>an incline motor control board listed as fitting the X22i and X32i was $145.99;</li>
            <li>a replacement console assembly for the older X15i incline trainer was $1,409.99.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In other words, a worn belt is a modest fix, but a failed console can cost more than the machine is worth. Test
            the screen, the incline through its whole range and the decline before you pay, and assume no warranty transfers.
            Our <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X22i review</a>, <a href="/nordictrack-x9i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X9i review</a> and <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> set out what to check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="x16-vs-x24" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>X16 or X24: Is the Bigger Screen Worth $600?</h2>
<p>
            On NordicTrack&rsquo;s figures the X16 and X24 are the same machine underneath: the same 22 by 60-inch belt, 4.25
            CHP motor, -6% to 40% grade, 400 lb rating, 69.9 by 38.1 by 73.3-inch footprint and warranty. The $600 difference
            on the 5 November checkout prices buys a 24-inch pivoting screen instead of a 16-inch one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pay it if iFIT is the reason you are buying, you will follow filmed routes every session, or you will turn the
            screen for floor classes, where a bigger picture is easier to follow from a mat. Otherwise the X16 is the better
            buy, and the $600 covers about a year and a half of iFIT Pro on the yearly plan or most of an extended warranty.
            Our <a href="/nordictrack-x24-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X24 review</a> compares the two
            in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Assembly, Space and Ceiling Height</h2>
<p>
            The hidden costs of an incline trainer are mostly about its size. Price these before you order.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Delivery and assembly.</strong> NordicTrack&rsquo;s X24 checkout listed threshold, room-of-choice and
            white-glove delivery and assembly on 5 November, but showed no prices for them, and estimated delivery at 10 to
            14 business days. The X24 is 445 lb in a 77 by 42 by 34-inch box, by NordicTrack&rsquo;s figures, so getting it down
            a basement stair is a job for professionals. On Amazon, the Sole listings on this page say they ship by freight,
            curbside only; check the terms on any incline trainer listing. Our <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a> explains the options.</li>
            <li><strong>Floor space.</strong> The X16 and X24 take 69.9 by 38.1 inches and do not fold, about 18.5 square feet
            before you add clearance behind the deck. See <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill dimensions and space</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Ceiling height.</strong> Treadmill Review Guru measured the X24&rsquo;s deck at 14 inches flat and
            31.5 inches at full incline, and Run to the Finish reports NordicTrack&rsquo;s guidance of the user&rsquo;s height plus
            three feet as a minimum ceiling. For a six-foot user that is nine feet, which most basements do not have. If yours
            is lower, the top of the range is unusable, and you have paid for incline you cannot reach. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-low-ceilings/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for low ceilings</a> guide covers the alternatives.</li>
            <li><strong>Wiring.</strong> Plan on a dedicated, grounded outlet with no extension lead; the commercial TRUE Alpine
            Runner calls for a dedicated 20-amp circuit. An electrician&rsquo;s visit is a cost to add if your room lacks one.</li>
            <li><strong>A mat.</strong> A heavy machine on a tilting deck works the floor hard; see <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill mats</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty and Repair Costs</h2>
<p>
            The X16 and X24 come with a <a href="/best-treadmills-with-long-warranty/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>lifetime motor warranty</a>, ten years on the frame, two years on parts and one year on
            labour, by NordicTrack&rsquo;s terms as summarised in our X24 review. NordicTrack also sells a three-year extended plan
            that raises those to 13, 5 and 4 years. On a machine whose whole point is a motorised deck that tilts through 46
            percentage points, two years of parts is short: the incline system and the console are the parts an incline
            trainer works hardest, and as the parts prices above show, a console can cost well over $1,000 out of warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            We could not find a price for the extended plan on 23 November, so ask for one at checkout and add it to your
            five-year sum. For the Amazon machines, check who is selling and whether the maker&rsquo;s warranty applies; the
            FreeMotion i11.9 listing states no warranty at all. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains what to look for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cheaper Alternatives: High-Incline Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            If what you want is hard uphill walking rather than 40% specifically, a high-incline treadmill gets you most of
            the way for a fraction of the price. On Amazon on 23 November 2026:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machine</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price (Amazon, 23 Nov)</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Incline</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Notes from the listing</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">THERUN 20% Auto Incline Treadmill</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$799.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–20%, powered</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph, 55 x 20 in. belt, 300 lb, folds</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F65</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,199.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–15%, powered</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph, 20 x 60 in. deck, folds, freight curbside</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-3% to 12%, powered</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph, 16 in. touchscreen, folds, iFIT Pro $39/mo</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack Commercial 2450</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,499</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-3% to 12%, powered</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">14 mph, 24 in. touchscreen, folds, iFIT Pro $39/mo</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The THERUN is the steepest powered treadmill with running speed we found on Amazon under $1,000: 20% for $799.99,
            against $3,699 for an X16. It has no decline and stops well short of 40%, but 20% is already far steeper than
            most people walk for long. Our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a> roundup compares the 15% to 20% machines, and <a href="/treadmills-with-the-highest-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with the highest incline</a> ranks machines by how much grade each price buys.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-needs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Pay for an Incline Trainer</h2>
<p>
            The price only makes sense if you will regularly use grades a normal treadmill cannot reach. That is a narrower
            group than the marketing suggests.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Worth it:</strong> hikers and mountain walkers who live somewhere flat; people who already walk at the
            top of a 15% treadmill and want more; and anyone who wants hard aerobic work at walking pace instead of running.
            Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-hiking/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for hiking</a> guide is written for this group.</li>
            <li><strong>Not worth it:</strong> runners, who gain nothing from 40% and lose top speed at steep grades; people
            doing routines such as <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30</a>,
            which needs only 12%; anyone with a ceiling under about nine feet; and anyone who needs a machine that folds.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A simple test: for a month, note the incline you actually use. If you keep hitting the top of your current
            treadmill&rsquo;s range and wishing for more, the premium buys you something every session. If not, it buys you a
            number on a spec sheet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When Incline Trainers Are Cheapest</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack runs promotions for much of the year, and our X24 review found lower figures, including $2,999.99, in
            search results for NordicTrack&rsquo;s own pages that we could not confirm on its checkout. Black Friday on
            27 November is the most likely time for a real cut. Judge any sale against the $3,699 and $4,299 checkout prices
            above, and check whether a deal depends on a bundled iFIT plan, a trade-in or a mailed code. Our <a href="/nordictrack-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Black Friday deals</a> page tracks the offers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Prices on Amazon can move the other way too: the Sunny Versatile rose by $800 between 6 and 23 November. If an
            Amazon incline trainer is on your list, check its price history before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing an Incline Trainer</h2>
          <p>Most come from pricing the machine and forgetting the room it has to fit in.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the membership</h3>
              <p>Five years of iFIT Pro is $1,980 to $2,340, more than half the price of an X16. Decide whether you want the classes before choosing a touchscreen trainer.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring the ceiling at level</h3>
              <p>The deck can rise to over 31 inches at full incline. Measure for the tallest user at the top of the grade, or you will pay for incline you cannot use.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying 40% to run</h3>
              <p>Speed is capped at steep grades and the X24 tops out at 12 mph. A runner gets more from a $2,499 Commercial 2450 or a 15% Sole.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving delivery unpriced</h3>
              <p>A 445 lb box needs room-of-choice or white-glove delivery. Ask for those prices, and any electrician work, before you compare totals.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A powered home incline trainer costs about $2,875 to $4,299 in November 2026</strong>,
            with the NordicTrack X16 at $3,699 and the X24 at $4,299, plus $39 a month for iFIT Pro. Over five years an X16
            with iFIT comes to roughly $5,700 to $6,000.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is worth paying only if you will walk above 15% most sessions and your ceiling can take the deck at full
            grade. If not, a 15% to 20% treadmill does the job for a third of the price; our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best incline treadmills</a> guide has the picks.
          </p>
        </div>

