        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does an incline trainer cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A powered home incline trainer costs about $2,875 to $4,299 in November 2026: $2,875 for the FreeMotion i11.9 and $2,999.99 for the Sunny Versatile on Amazon (23 November), and $3,699 for the NordicTrack X16 and $4,299 for the X24 on NordicTrack's checkout (5 November). Commercial models cost $10,000 or more.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a NordicTrack X22i?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The X22i is no longer sold new: NordicTrack has replaced it and the X32i with the X16 ($3,699) and X24 ($4,299). A used X22i's fair price depends on age and condition; test the console, incline and decline before buying, because a replacement console for an older X-series trainer can cost over $1,400.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do you need iFIT for a NordicTrack incline trainer?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not to use it as a treadmill, but the classes and filmed routes that move the deck automatically need iFIT Pro, which is $39 a month or $396 a year at iFIT's regular prices on 23 November 2026. Over five years that is $1,980 to $2,340 on top of the machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is an incline trainer worth the extra money over a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if you will regularly walk above 15%. An X16 costs $1,700 more than a NordicTrack Commercial 1750 with the same screen size, and the extra buys 40% incline, a -6% decline and a 400 lb frame, not a better running treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the cheapest incline trainer?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The cheapest machines reaching 40% are manual walking treadmills such as the LONTEK ($195.49) and CURSOR ($249.99) on Amazon on 23 November 2026, but you set their incline by hand. The cheapest powered 30% trainer we found was the FreeMotion i11.9 at $2,875.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How tall a ceiling does an incline trainer need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Run to the Finish reports NordicTrack's guidance as the user's height plus three feet, so about nine feet for a six-foot user. The X24's deck rises from about 14 inches flat to 31.5 inches at full incline in Treadmill Review Guru's measurement.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

